  "description_html": "Simple diagram depicting possible amouts of <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Feather\" title=\"Feather\">feathers</a> in <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Therizinosauroidea\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Therizinosauroidea\">Therizinosauroid</a> <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Dinosaurs\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Dinosaurs\">dinosaurs</a>. <b>A.</b> Lack of feathers. While at least some Therizinosaurs (like <i><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://people.eku.edu/ritchisong/554images/Beiapiosaurus.jpg\">Beipiaosaurus</a></i>, see also <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"http://www.most.gov.cn/eng/newsletters/2009/200903/W020090320455028779127.jpg\">[1]</a> and <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"http://4.bp.blogspot.com/_5jvcMWhYJGc/SWxUnZ2YMMI/AAAAAAAAARg/f_cENlKb-Hg/s400/Beipaiosaurus.jpg\">[2]</a>) had feathers, it is not impossible the largest genera (like <i>[Therizinosaurus]</i>) lacked them, and had <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=Scales&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Scales (page does not exist)\">scales</a>. <b>B.</b> Thick coat with filamentous feathers, as it may looked at the living <i>Beipiaosaurus</i> <b>C.</b> As some genera may could have looked: body covered with scales, but with feathers on the tail, neck and forelimbs.",
